            <rel name="summary" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="volitional-cause" type="rst"/>
        </relations>
    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="23" relname="span">I tend to believe that intuitiveness is partly learned.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="3" relname="background">Take the light switch as an example.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="36" relname="span">The on-off switch is a fairly basic design.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="35" relname="span">However, designers have changed the light switch over the years to make it more appealing and to bring more functionality to it.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="4" relname="elaboration">These days a light switch can be a round wheel that has to be pushed in to turn on the light.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="35" relname="elaboration">The same switch can be rotated to dim the lights or make them brighter.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="8" relname="concession">While simplicity is still there,</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="41" relname="span">the user experience with this light switch is different,</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="37" relname="span">and it might be argued that it has to be learned.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="40" relname="antithesis">One might decide to try to "pull out" the light switch to toggle on,</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="40" relname="span">rather than push in,</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="11" relname="elaboration">or one might think just turning the wheel will turn it on or off.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="39" relname="background">Actually, I think some of these switches do work differently depending on the design.</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="43" relname="span">The bottom line is not everything, including light switches, are necessarily intuitive.</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="26" relname="span">Bad design, says Don Norman, can get in our way of using every day things.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="30" relname="background">Norman uses the examples of doors.</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="30" relname="span">In some fancy hotels, it is unclear whether one is to push in a glass door or pull the handle.</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="19" relname="nonvolitional-cause">Some doors do not have knobs or handles,</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="31" relname="span">so which direction does one push or pull?</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="32" relname="span">Again, part of it is about fancy design getting in the way of usability.</segment>
        <segment id="21" parent="29" relname="span">Web sites are probably the worst violators of all</segment>
        <segment id="22" parent="21" relname="nonvolitional-cause">given design is often a free for all.</segment>
        <group id="23" type="span"/>
        <group id="24" type="span" parent="1" relname="evidence"/>
        <group id="25" type="span" parent="24" relname="span"/>
        <group id="26" type="span" parent="14" relname="nonvolitional-cause"/>
        <group id="27" type="span" parent="15" relname="elaboration"/>
